<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hungarian invasions of Europe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 899–970</span> · Also known as Hungarian raids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hungarian invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> were the raids made by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> from the Carpathian Basin into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> between about 899 and 970. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">Mounted archers</a> struck as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, southern France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The raids were checked by Otto I's victory over the Hungarians at the Lechfeld in 955 and ended after a Byzantine victory at Arcadiopolis in 970, after which the Hungarians formed a settled Christian kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hungarian-invasions-of-europe.jpg" alt="Hungarian invasions of Europe" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: hu:User:Csanády · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalandozasok.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
